1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,064
In 1977, scientists found dense animal
life where no sunlight has ever reached.

2
00:00:06,064 --> 00:00:11,098
That year, the submersible Alvin, operated
by Woods Hole Oceanographic Institution,

3
00:00:11,098 --> 00:00:15,950
dove to hot springs on the Galápagos Rift
and found clams, mussels and tubeworms

4
00:00:15,950 --> 00:00:17,163
crowded around them.

5
00:00:17,163 --> 00:00:19,514
The explanation is chemosynthesis.

6
00:00:19,514 --> 00:00:25,255
Chemosynthesis is how some microbes make
food from carbon dioxide using energy from

7
00:00:25,255 --> 00:00:27,952
chemical reactions instead of sunlight.

8
00:00:27,952 --> 00:00:31,715
That answer is correct, and it hides an
assumption worth breaking.

9
00:00:31,715 --> 00:00:34,965
The animals on that rift were not living
without the sun.

10
00:00:34,965 --> 00:00:37,831
Most of them were still breathing its
leftovers.

11
00:00:37,831 --> 00:00:42,248
To see why, you have to follow the
chemistry, and then follow the evidence

12
00:00:42,248 --> 00:00:43,323
for the chemistry.

13
00:00:43,323 --> 00:00:46,065
Start with what any living cell needs.

14
00:00:46,065 --> 00:00:51,405
It needs carbon to build its body, and it
needs energy to do the building.

15
00:00:51,405 --> 00:00:56,061
A plant gets its energy from light and its
carbon from carbon dioxide.

16
00:00:56,061 --> 00:01:01,362
A chemosynthetic microbe takes the same
carbon dioxide, but it gets its energy

17
00:01:01,362 --> 00:01:02,381
somewhere else.

18
00:01:02,381 --> 00:01:07,352
NOAA describes the process in exactly
these terms: microbes harvest energy from

19
00:01:07,352 --> 00:01:11,443
chemical reactions and use it to turn
carbon into organic matter.

20
00:01:11,443 --> 00:01:16,001
The reactions involve molecules that are,
chemically speaking, loaded.

21
00:01:16,001 --> 00:01:18,605
Hydrogen sulfide is the classic example.

22
00:01:18,605 --> 00:01:23,876
It carries electrons it will readily give
up to oxygen, and when that hand-off

23
00:01:23,876 --> 00:01:25,768
happens, energy is released.

24
00:01:25,768 --> 00:01:29,967
The microbe captures some of that energy
before it escapes as heat.

25
00:01:29,967 --> 00:01:34,040
That is the whole trick: chemosynthesis
makes food without light.

26
00:01:34,040 --> 00:01:38,389
So the difference from photosynthesis sits
in one place, the first step.

27
00:01:38,389 --> 00:01:41,439
Photosynthesis captures energy from
sunlight.

28
00:01:41,439 --> 00:01:45,167
Chemosynthesis captures energy from
chemical reactions.

29
00:01:45,167 --> 00:01:49,640
After that first step the two converge
more than you might expect.

30
00:01:49,640 --> 00:01:55,016
Many chemosynthetic bacteria fix carbon
with the same cycle plants use, the Calvin

31
00:01:55,016 --> 00:01:59,277
cycle, driven by the same carbon-grabbing
enzyme, called RuBisCO.

32
00:01:59,277 --> 00:02:02,170
Others use different carbon-fixing
pathways.

33
00:02:02,170 --> 00:02:07,166
Either way, the microbe ends up with
sugars and other organic molecules, and

34
00:02:07,166 --> 00:02:11,571
anything that eats the microbe, or houses
it, inherits that carbon.

35
00:02:11,571 --> 00:02:15,405
That is the base of a food chain. The
waste products differ too.

36
00:02:15,405 --> 00:02:20,502
A sulfide-oxidizing microbe can leave
behind elemental sulfur, sometimes stored

37
00:02:20,502 --> 00:02:22,824
as visible granules inside the cell.

38
00:02:22,824 --> 00:02:25,947
Hold on to that detail. It turns out to be
a clue.

39
00:02:25,947 --> 00:02:26,651
Why vents?

40
00:02:26,651 --> 00:02:32,075
Because they manufacture exactly the
mismatch a chemosynthetic microbe needs.

41
00:02:32,075 --> 00:02:36,206
At ocean ridges, seawater seeps into the
crust through cracks.

42
00:02:36,206 --> 00:02:41,871
Deep down, near hot rock and magma, heated
rock strips out oxygen from that water and

43
00:02:41,871 --> 00:02:44,869
lets it dissolve metals and sulfur
compounds.

44
00:02:44,869 --> 00:02:47,273
The fluid gains hydrogen sulfide.

45
00:02:47,273 --> 00:02:51,061
Then, buoyant and hot, it rises and gushes
back out.

46
00:02:51,061 --> 00:02:55,905
At the vent opening, hot fluid meets cold
oxygenated seawater.

47
00:02:55,905 --> 00:02:58,405
Neither water alone is much use.

48
00:02:58,405 --> 00:03:02,161
The vent fluid has fuel but nothing to
burn it with.

49
00:03:02,161 --> 00:03:05,483
The ocean water has oxygen but almost no
fuel.

50
00:03:05,483 --> 00:03:10,420
Where they mix, in a narrow and constantly
shifting zone, microbes sit in the gap and

51
00:03:10,420 --> 00:03:11,408
run the reaction.

52
00:03:11,408 --> 00:03:16,392
The hottest vent fluid reaches hundreds of
degrees Celsius, and the published figures

53
00:03:16,392 --> 00:03:20,320
vary widely from site to site, so no
single number tells the story.

54
00:03:20,320 --> 00:03:23,779
The microbes do not live in the hottest
fluid anyway.

55
00:03:23,779 --> 00:03:26,651
They live where it has cooled enough to
mix.

56
00:03:26,651 --> 00:03:31,709
That detail matters, because it tells you
where to look for the evidence: not in the

57
00:03:31,709 --> 00:03:36,587
plume itself, but on the surfaces, mats
and animal tissues bathed in the mixture.

58
00:03:36,587 --> 00:03:38,474
Reality is usually available.

59
00:03:38,474 --> 00:03:42,053
People keep choosing the version that's
easier to sell.

60
00:03:42,053 --> 00:03:47,424
Before 1977, the working picture of the
deep sea floor was one of scarcity.

61
00:03:47,424 --> 00:03:52,551
Food arrived from above as a slow fall of
dead plankton and waste, often called

62
00:03:52,551 --> 00:03:55,536
marine snow, and life thinned out
accordingly.

63
00:03:55,536 --> 00:03:58,686
The Galápagos Rift communities broke that
budget.

64
00:03:58,686 --> 00:04:04,021
There were simply too many animals, packed
too densely, for falling scraps to feed.

65
00:04:04,021 --> 00:04:08,975
The expedition was led largely by
geologists, who had come to find hot

66
00:04:08,975 --> 00:04:11,099
springs, not to study animals.

67
00:04:11,099 --> 00:04:17,291
The biology was a surprise. And the
density itself was the first piece of

68
00:04:17,291 --> 00:04:20,276
It meant there had to be a local food
source.

69
00:04:20,276 --> 00:04:25,317
It did not yet say which organisms made
the food, or how the animals got it.

70
00:04:25,317 --> 00:04:29,471
A local source in total darkness could not
be photosynthesis.

71
00:04:29,471 --> 00:04:34,645
The vent water smelled of hydrogen
sulfide, and microbiologists already knew

72
00:04:34,645 --> 00:04:37,709
some bacteria could live on sulfur
compounds.

73
00:04:37,709 --> 00:04:39,945
Chemosynthesis itself was not new.

74
00:04:39,945 --> 00:04:45,403
Sergei Winogradsky had described bacteria
living on inorganic chemicals in the late

75
00:04:45,403 --> 00:04:49,480
nineteenth century, working with sulfur
bacteria from springs.

76
00:04:49,480 --> 00:04:54,807
What was new was the suggestion that a
whole animal community, on the sea floor,

77
00:04:54,807 --> 00:04:55,939
could rest on it.

78
00:04:55,939 --> 00:04:59,976
That was a hypothesis, and it needed more
than a smell to stand on.

79
00:04:59,976 --> 00:05:04,536
The animal that made the case was the
giant tubeworm, Riftia pachyptila.

80
00:05:04,536 --> 00:05:08,693
Its body sits inside a white tube, topped
by a bright red plume.

81
00:05:08,693 --> 00:05:13,669
When biologists dissected adults, they
found something odd: no mouth and no gut.

82
00:05:13,669 --> 00:05:16,764
An animal that large cannot live on
nothing.

83
00:05:16,764 --> 00:05:22,110
Inside, though, was a spongy organ, later
called the trophosome, and it held

84
00:05:22,110 --> 00:05:24,149
crystals of elemental sulfur.

85
00:05:24,149 --> 00:05:28,586
In 1981, Colleen Cavanaugh, then a
graduate student, proposed that the

86
00:05:28,586 --> 00:05:33,466
trophosome was full of bacteria, and that
those bacteria were chemosynthetic,

87
00:05:33,466 --> 00:05:35,557
feeding the worm from the inside.

88
00:05:35,557 --> 00:05:36,864
Notice the reasoning.

89
00:05:36,864 --> 00:05:41,653
The sulfur crystals matched the expected
waste of sulfide-oxidizing microbes.

90
00:05:41,653 --> 00:05:44,481
The missing gut ruled out the ordinary
route.

91
00:05:44,481 --> 00:05:49,446
The idea connected two facts that, on
their own, explained nothing, and pointed

92
00:05:49,446 --> 00:05:51,835
straight at the bacteria that feed it.

93
00:05:51,835 --> 00:05:53,764
The plume completes the picture.

94
00:05:53,764 --> 00:05:58,043
It is red because it is full of
hemoglobin, but a specialised form that

95
00:05:58,043 --> 00:06:02,745
carries oxygen and sulfide together,
binding the sulfide so it does not poison

96
00:06:02,745 --> 00:06:04,011
the worm's own cells.

97
00:06:04,011 --> 00:06:07,011
The worm, in other words, is a delivery
system.

98
00:06:07,011 --> 00:06:12,055
It reaches into the mixing zone, loads
both ingredients from the water, and its

99
00:06:12,055 --> 00:06:15,056
blood carries them down to the bacteria
inside.

100
00:06:15,056 --> 00:06:18,581
The plume is not a mouth. It is a gill
doing double duty.

101
00:06:18,581 --> 00:06:22,162
The bacteria do the chemistry. The worm
handles logistics.

102
00:06:22,162 --> 00:06:26,606
Evidence also indicates that young worms
pick up their bacteria from the

103
00:06:26,606 --> 00:06:31,545
surrounding water rather than inheriting
them, so each generation has to recruit

104
00:06:31,545 --> 00:06:32,656
its partners anew.

105
00:06:32,656 --> 00:06:36,656
A plausible story is where evidence
starts, not where it ends.

106
00:06:36,656 --> 00:06:41,688
The symbiosis was tested along several
separate lines, and the strength of the

107
00:06:41,688 --> 00:06:44,075
conclusion comes from how they agree.

108
00:06:44,075 --> 00:06:45,470
First, microscopy.

109
00:06:45,470 --> 00:06:51,749
Thin sections of trophosome tissue showed
it densely packed with bacterial cells.

110
00:06:51,749 --> 00:06:53,511
Second, enzyme activity.

111
00:06:53,511 --> 00:06:59,455
Researchers measured enzymes involved in
carbon fixation and in sulfur metabolism

112
00:06:59,455 --> 00:07:05,547
in trophosome tissue, enzymes an animal on
its own would not be expected to supply.

113
00:07:05,547 --> 00:07:10,117
That showed the machinery was present and
working, not just the cells.

114
00:07:10,117 --> 00:07:11,551
Third, carbon isotopes.

115
00:07:11,551 --> 00:07:16,538
Carbon comes in a lighter and a heavier
stable form, and different carbon-fixing

116
00:07:16,538 --> 00:07:19,904
routes take them up in slightly different
proportions.

117
00:07:19,904 --> 00:07:23,995
That leaves a fingerprint, measured as a
ratio of heavy to light carbon.

118
00:07:23,995 --> 00:07:28,668
In vent animals, the signature differs
from plankton-derived food sinking from

119
00:07:28,668 --> 00:07:29,387
the surface.

120
00:07:29,387 --> 00:07:34,117
That is how you can say the carbon was
fixed locally rather than imported.

121
00:07:34,117 --> 00:07:35,506
Fourth, DNA sequencing.

122
00:07:35,506 --> 00:07:40,033
The tubeworm's symbionts have resisted
being grown in the lab, so they were

123
00:07:40,033 --> 00:07:44,742
identified by their genes, and genomic
work suggests some symbionts carry more

124
00:07:44,742 --> 00:07:46,613
than one carbon-fixing pathway.

125
00:07:46,613 --> 00:07:50,732
No single one of these independent lines
of evidence proves the case.

126
00:07:50,732 --> 00:07:54,373
Together they leave very little room for
another explanation.

127
00:07:54,373 --> 00:07:58,773
Sulfide at hot vents is the famous
example, but it is not the only one.

128
00:07:58,773 --> 00:08:03,636
At cold seeps, fluids rich in methane and
sulfide leak from sediments along

129
00:08:03,636 --> 00:08:04,933
continental margins.

130
00:08:04,933 --> 00:08:07,442
There is no volcanic heat required.

131
00:08:07,442 --> 00:08:12,960
Researchers at MBARI, the Monterey Bay
Aquarium Research Institute, have used

132
00:08:12,960 --> 00:08:18,693
remotely operated vehicles to study seeps
off the California coast, where clams,

133
00:08:18,693 --> 00:08:23,064
tubeworms and microbial mats gather much
as they do at vents.

134
00:08:23,064 --> 00:08:26,766
Some seep mussels host bacteria that use
methane itself.

135
00:08:26,766 --> 00:08:32,054
Strictly, that is a slightly different
metabolism, because the carbon comes from

136
00:08:32,054 --> 00:08:36,747
methane rather than carbon dioxide, but it
is usually counted under the

137
00:08:36,747 --> 00:08:38,333
chemosynthetic umbrella.

138
00:08:38,333 --> 00:08:42,381
In sediments with no oxygen at all,
partnerships of archaea and

139
00:08:42,381 --> 00:08:47,327
sulfate-reducing bacteria break down
methane together, a job neither seems to

140
00:08:47,327 --> 00:08:48,227
do well alone.

141
00:08:48,227 --> 00:08:53,416
Elsewhere, microbes draw energy from
hydrogen, from reduced iron, and from

142
00:08:53,416 --> 00:08:58,720
Ammonia-oxidizing microbes are spread
through the dark open ocean, far from any

143
00:08:58,720 --> 00:09:00,600
vent, quietly fixing carbon.

144
00:09:00,600 --> 00:09:05,287
How much carbon chemosynthesis fixes in
the open ocean, as a share of the global

145
00:09:05,287 --> 00:09:08,275
total, is still being measured, and
estimates vary.

146
00:09:08,275 --> 00:09:12,856
Even a whale carcass on the sea floor can
host the process, as bacteria break down

147
00:09:12,856 --> 00:09:16,376
the bones and release sulfide that other
microbes then oxidize.

148
00:09:16,376 --> 00:09:19,006
Now return to the assumption from the
opening.

149
00:09:19,006 --> 00:09:23,123
Vent communities are often described as
living entirely without the sun.

150
00:09:23,123 --> 00:09:24,371
That is half right.

151
00:09:24,371 --> 00:09:29,891
They are independent of sunlight as an
energy source, because no light reaches

152
00:09:29,891 --> 00:09:32,644
They are not independent of
photosynthesis.

153
00:09:32,644 --> 00:09:37,637
The most common vent reaction oxidizes
sulfide using dissolved oxygen, and the

154
00:09:37,637 --> 00:09:42,567
oxygen in deep seawater was produced by
photosynthesis near the surface, then

155
00:09:42,567 --> 00:09:44,744
carried down by ocean circulation.

156
00:09:44,744 --> 00:09:49,689
Take away the sun, and over time the deep
ocean's oxygen supply would run down, and

157
00:09:49,689 --> 00:09:51,715
with it most of these communities.

158
00:09:51,715 --> 00:09:56,435
The tubeworm's plume is, in a sense,
reaching for sunlight that fell long ago.

159
00:09:56,435 --> 00:10:00,099
There are exceptions, and they are where
the research gets most interesting.

160
00:10:00,099 --> 00:10:05,091
Some microbes make methane from hydrogen
and carbon dioxide with no oxygen

161
00:10:05,091 --> 00:10:09,949
involved, and some vent systems release
enough hydrogen to support them.

162
00:10:09,949 --> 00:10:15,127
Those communities come closest to running
on Earth's internal chemistry alone.

163
00:10:15,127 --> 00:10:19,309
That possibility is one reason NASA
supports research on vents.

164
00:10:19,309 --> 00:10:23,994
If life can run on rock and water
chemistry here, the same might hold on

165
00:10:23,994 --> 00:10:28,157
ocean worlds elsewhere. That is a reason
to look, not a finding.

166
00:10:28,157 --> 00:10:32,817
Watch for footage of black smoke billowing
from a chimney, captioned as the source of

167
00:10:32,817 --> 00:10:33,091
life.

168
00:10:33,091 --> 00:10:38,419
That black cloud is mineral particles,
forming as hot fluid hits cold water.

169
00:10:38,419 --> 00:10:43,851
The microbes doing chemosynthesis are
mostly invisible: in thin mats on rock, in

170
00:10:43,851 --> 00:10:46,499
the mixing water, inside animal tissue.

171
00:10:46,499 --> 00:10:51,489
When an animation shows a sulfide molecule
drifting straight into a worm, it

172
00:10:51,489 --> 00:10:52,605
compresses a lot.

173
00:10:52,605 --> 00:10:57,361
The real hand-off runs through a blood
protein, a specialised organ and a

174
00:10:57,361 --> 00:10:58,339
bacterial cell.

175
00:10:58,339 --> 00:11:03,126
And when a graphic says a vent ecosystem
runs without the sun, you now know which

176
00:11:03,126 --> 00:11:04,189
caveat it dropped.

177
00:11:04,189 --> 00:11:07,620
The tubeworm case is solid, but it reaches
only so far.

178
00:11:07,620 --> 00:11:12,111
It shows how chemosynthesis works
chemically, and it shows that specific

179
00:11:12,111 --> 00:11:13,421
animals depend on it.

180
00:11:13,421 --> 00:11:17,727
It does not tell you how much carbon
chemosynthesis fixes in the open ocean,

181
00:11:17,727 --> 00:11:20,616
where the microbes are diffuse and hard to
measure.

182
00:11:20,616 --> 00:11:25,324
It does not tell you how many vent and
seep sites exist, because most of the

183
00:11:25,324 --> 00:11:27,555
ridge system has never been visited.

184
00:11:27,555 --> 00:11:32,178
Many symbionts have never been grown in
the lab, so much of what is known about

185
00:11:32,178 --> 00:11:36,040
their metabolism is read from genes rather
than watched in action.

186
00:11:36,040 --> 00:11:39,597
And it does not settle whether life began
at vents.

187
00:11:39,597 --> 00:11:43,224
That is a serious idea, still debated, and
unproven.

188
00:11:43,224 --> 00:11:46,315
So chemosynthesis is not life without the
sun.

189
00:11:46,315 --> 00:11:51,221
It is life that found a second way to
spend it: oxygen made in the light,

190
00:11:51,221 --> 00:11:56,799
meeting fuel made in the rock, in a thin
layer of mixing water at the bottom of the

191
00:11:56,799 --> 00:12:01,235
ocean, where a microbe stands in the gap
and keeps the difference.
